        "output": "Title: 2019 B1: QBID Update\nURL: https://resources.taxschool.illinois.edu/taxbookarchive/2019/B1_QBID_Update.pdf\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: University of Illinois Tax School - dbh\nHighlights:\n, a calendar-year taxpayer, is the sole owner of a rental property and actively participates in its management. During 2018, Alan spent 60 hours approving new tenants, deciding on rental terms, and approving expenditures. Contractors performed\n\n---\n\nTitle: Federal tax engine documentation\nURL: https://quantcalc.app/docs/federal-tax/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nQuantCalc computes each year's federal income tax with one engine: a 2026 Form 1040 composed from the year's income, rule by rule, in the order the form computes it. The planner uses it for every simulated year, and the federal tax API runs it on a single return. These pages document every step it takes, the form line each value lands on, the official source of each rule (157 distinct documents, each quote re-checked weekly) and the tests that prove it. Engine version 2.5.1.\n\n...\n\n- Income: Form 1040 lines 2a to 8. Tax-exempt interest; IRA distributions and Roth conversions; Pensions and annuities; Social Security benefits; Taxable Social Security (the Pub 915 worksheet); Other ordinary income; Rental real estate: the passive activity loss limit (Form 8582); Rental real estate income or loss (Schedule E line 26, Schedule 1 line 5); Taxable interest (line 2b); Ordinary dividends (line 3b); Partnerships, S corporations, estates and trusts (Schedule E Parts II-III) and Form 8582's other passive activities.\n- Capital gains: Schedule D netting. Net short-term gain or loss; Net long-term gain or loss; Combined net gain or loss; Capital-gain distributions (Schedule D line 13); 28% rate gain (Schedule D line 18); Unrecaptured section 1250 gain (Schedule D line 19); Line 7a: the net gain, or a net loss; Short-term capital loss carryover in (Schedule D line 6); Long-term capital loss carryover in (Schedule D line 14); Capital loss allowed (Schedule D line 21); Short-term capital loss carryover to next year; Long-term capital loss carryover to next year.\n- Work income: wages, self-employment tax, the Additional Medicare Tax, tips, overtime and the QBI deduction. Wages (Form 1040 line 1a); Business income (Schedule 1 line 3); Self-employment tax (Schedule SE, Schedule 2 line 4); Deductible part of self-employment tax (Schedule 1 line 15); Self-employed retirement plan deduction (Schedule 1 line 16); Self-employed health insurance deduction (Schedule 1 line 17); Additional Medicare Tax (Form 8959); Excess social security tax withheld (Schedule 3 line 11); Employee payroll taxes (FICA), a standalone function; The Social Security earnings test, a standalone function.\n- Total income and adjusted gross income. Adjustments to income (Form 1040 line 10); Total income; Adjusted gross income.\n- Deductions: the standard deduction, §63(f) and the senior deduction. Standard deduction and the §63(f) aged amount; Additional standard deduction for blindness; The senior deduction (Schedule 1-A); Tips received in a trade or business (section 224(c)); No tax on tips (Schedule 1-A Part II); No tax on overtime (Schedule 1-A Part III); Qualified business income deduction (Forms 8995 / 8995-A); Additional deductions on line 13b; Total deductions; Taxable income.\n- Tax: the brackets and the capital-gains worksheet. Tax: the brackets and the capital-gains worksheet; Tax: the Tax Table below $100,000; Tax: the Schedule D Tax Worksheet; Qualified dividends (line 3a).\n- The alternative minimum tax and the foreign tax credit. Foreign tax credit without Form 1116 (the section 904(j) election); Foreign tax credit with Form 1116 (the section 904(a) limit, passive category); Alternative minimum tax (Form 6251); The AMT foreign tax credit (Form 6251 line 8).\n- Credits: the credit for the elderly or disabled, the saver's credit, the child tax credit and the earned income credit. Form 1040 lines 18 to 22: the order of the nonrefundable credits; Credit for the elderly or the disabled (Schedule R); Saver's credit (Form 8880); Child tax credit, credit for other dependents and additional child tax credit (Schedule 8812); Earned income credit (Form 1040 line 27a); The premium tax credit (Form 8962) and the net credit (Schedule 3 line 9).\n- Other taxes: §72(t) and the NIIT. The 10% additional tax on early distributions; Net investment income tax; Other taxes; Total tax; Total investment income (Form 8960 line 8); Net investment income: nonqualified annuities (Form 8960 line 3); Net investment income: rents (Form 8960 line 4a); Form 8960 line 9a: investment interest; Form 8960 line 9b: state income tax allocable to net investment income; Form 8960 line 12: net investment income; Excess advance premium tax credit repayment (Form 8962 line 29, Schedule 2 line 1a); Net investment income: the K-1 trade or business amounts (Form 8960 line 4b); Form 5329 Part III: excess traditional IRA contributions; Form 5329 Part IV: excess Roth IRA contributions; Form 5329 Part V: excess Coverdell ESA contributions; Form 5329 Part VI: excess Archer MSA contributions; Form 5329 Part VII: excess HSA contributions; Form 5329 Part VIII: excess ABLE contributions; Form 5329 Part X: excess Trump account contributions; Form 5329 Part XI: 100% of a distributed Trump account excess's earnings.\n- Itemized deductions: Schedule A, §68 and the non-itemizer charitable deduction. Medical and dental expenses; State and local taxes paid; The SALT cap and its phase-down; Interest you paid; Gifts to charity: the percentage limits and the 0.5% floor; Gifts to charity: the five-year carryover; The §68 limitation on itemized deductions; Total itemized deductions; Itemize or take the standard deduction; Charitable deduction for non-itemizers.\n- Retirement accounts: Form 8606 basis, Roth distributions, Form 5329 and the RMD. Form 8606: the nontaxable part of a conversion; Form 8606: the nontaxable part of all distributions; Form 8606: basis carried forward; Roth IRA distributions: ordering and the 5-year rules; Form 5329: early distributions; Form 5329: the exceptions to the additional tax; Form 5329: the missed-RMD excise tax; The RMD divisor: Uniform Lifetime or Joint and Last Survivor; The required minimum distribution; An inherited account: the payout rule and the distribution period; An inherited account: the year's required distribution; §72(q): the 10% on a nonqualified annuity before 59½; §72(q)(2): the annuity's exceptions.\n- Estimated tax: the §6654 safe harbors and the Form 2210 penalty. Form 2210: the required annual payment; Form 2210: the underpayment by installment; Form 2210: the penalty; Form 2210 Schedule AI: the annualized income installment method.\n- Later tax years: the projection beyond 2026. One amount in a later year; The year's brackets and deductions; The IRMAA income floors in a later year; The QCD limits in a later year.\n\nIn the planner (/api/simulate and the app) every simulated year runs the base return: the income, capital-gain, AGI, deduction, tax and other-tax steps, with the standard deduction, the §63(f) aged amount and the senior deduction (no blindness amount) or, when the plan enters itemized deductions (mortgage interest, property tax, charitable gifts, medical costs), Schedule A with that year's own state and local income tax on line 5a, taken when it gives the lower tax (2026-10-12), taxable-account interest and dividends when the plan sets a taxable yield (it defaults to 0), and the capital-loss deduction with its carryover on each simulated path. The retirement-account and estimated-tax steps run on the federal tax API and in the library only: the planner's own required minimum distributions use the Uniform Lifetime Table (each spouse's own balance, age and divisor ... The ACA bridge optimizer also applies the Roth ordering rules and the Joint and Last Survivor Table; the planner applies the Roth ordering and 5-year rules and Form 8606 basis when the plan gives a Roth contribution basis, the 5-year clock's first year or an IRA basis. The planner and the bridge compute line 16 on the rate schedules in every year, not the Tax Table the federal tax API uses below $100,000 (a difference of at most $6 in a year). In the planner a QCD is capped per IRA owner (a joint return with both spouses 70½ or older may exclude twice the limit; the indexed limit is rounded to the nearest $1,000), a separate filer who lived apart from the spouse all year pays IRMAA on the individual table, and the RMD divides the balance before the year's returns (engine 1.5.0). Federal engine 2.1: a Rental income life event is rental real estate on each year's return (Schedule E through Form 8582: the passive-activity loss limit with active participation, the carryforward on each path; net investment income; the state return takes Schedule E line 26 as other income, a loss down to that income's zero). The planner figures the credits in a year with wages or self-employment income (federal engine 2.2): the earned income credit (with qualifying children only when the plan lists dependents, whose child tax credit and credit for other dependents every retirement year figures; refundable, so the year's federal total can be a refund), the saver's credit on the plan's contributions of the year before retirement (the primary's; through 2026), and the credit for the elderly, which cannot lower the tax of a filer 65 or older on the standard deduction (PROP-F21-R-1); other years have no earned income and no contribution, so no credit.\n\n...\n\n- Form 5329 Part II (the additional tax on Coverdell ESA, QTP and ABLE account distributions), and around Parts III-VIII (federal engine 2.3): the deemed contribution of a prior excess absorbed by this year's unused limit (the IRA Deduction Worksheet, Form 8889 line 13: the engine has no traditional IRA or HSA deduction), Form 8889 itself (the HSA deduction, the 20% on distributions not used for medical expenses), the contributors' Coverdell MAGI limits, the Archer MSA limit and the ABLE-to-work amount (the caller's limits), a spouse's Coverdell ESA, Archer MSA, ABLE or Trump account, the 2025 Trump account excess (0 for 2026) and the excess-contribution limits of a projected year (refused) Source: IRS f5329.pdf (“Distributions included in income from a Coverdell ESA, a QTP, or an ABLE account”); irs.gov/pub/irs-dft/i5329--dft.pdf (“Also include on your 2026 Form 8889, line 13, the smaller of:”).\n- Royalty income, Schedule E Parts IV-V (REMICs, farm rental), Form 4797 gains, farm income and personal-property rental: no field carries them into the net investment income tax (otherOrdinaryIncome and pension are never net investment income; a nonqualified annuity has its own field; rental real estate has rentalNetIncome, federal engine 2.1; partnerships, S corporations, estates and trusts the K-1 fields, federal engine 2.3) Source: law.cornell.edu IRC §1411 (“gross income from interest, dividends, annuities, royalties, and rents”); IRS f1040s1.pdf (“Rental real estate, royalties, partnerships, S corporations, trusts, etc.”).\n- Other Schedule 1 income items with their own rules: the net operating loss deduction, hobby income, §951/§951A inclusions, the §461(l) excess business loss adjustment and digital assets received as ordinary income Source: IRS f1040s1.pdf (“Net operating loss”); IRS f1040s1.pdf (“Digital assets received as ordinary income”).\n- The foreign earned income and housing exclusions (Form 2555) and the possession exclusions: MAGI is AGI throughout (the senior deduction, the NIIT, the SALT cap), and there is no Foreign Earned Income Tax Worksheet Source: IRS f1040s1.pdf (“Foreign earned income exclusion from Form 2555”).\n\n...\n\n- Rental real estate beyond Schedule E Part I as one net amount, and Schedule E Parts II-III beyond one net amount per class (federal engine 2.3): royalties, REMICs (Part IV), farm rental (Form 4835), the release of a suspended loss on a disposition (section 469(g)) or at death, the commercial revitalization deduction, the per-activity allocation of the unallowed loss within a class (Form 8582 worksheets: each class is one activity), depreciation (an input inside the net amount) and its recapture, the at-risk rules (Form 6198) and the partner and shareholder basis limits (sections 704(d), 1366(d); Forms 7203/7217: the K-1 amounts are entered after them), the excess business loss limitation of section 461(l) (refused above its threshold), publicly traded partnerships, self-charged interest, the Form 1041 K-1 box 14 code H adjustment of Form 8960 line 7, a net operating loss, the rental real estate safe harbor of the QBI deduction (Rev. ... section 1.1411-4(g)(7)); Form 1116 line 3e takes the positive rental net income as gross income Source: IRS f8582.pdf (“Total losses allowed from all passive activities”).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Rental Tax Issues Worth Watching | Texas Real Estate Research Center\nURL: https://trerc.tamu.edu/article/rental-tax-issues-2308/\nPublished: 2021-06-16T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nreal estate activity enables the taxpayer to deduct up to $25,000 of losses from rental real estate under certain circumstances free of the bar of passive loss limitations ... ’s operations. To be an active participant in a rental real estate activity\n\n---\n\nTitle: Real Estate Tax Rules Explained: Material… | Doeren Mayhew\nURL: https://www.doeren.com/viewpoint/real-estate-tax-rules-explained-material-participation-qualified-business-income-and-net-investment-income-tax\nPublished: 2026-02-10T13:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nQBI asks whether income is from a qualified trade or business (and whether you meet the wage/property limits and other rules). For rentals, many taxpayers look to the IRS rental real estate safe harbor, which can treat a rental real estate enterprise as a trade or business solely for QBI, if requirements are met (including separate books and records, 250 hours of rental services and contemporaneous recordkeeping). Meeting or failing the QBI safe harbor does not decide passive versus non-passive treatment.\n\n---\n\nTitle: September 2025\nURL: https://proadvance.taxact.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/TaxAct-Gig-Tax-Savings-Short-Term-Rentals-Sep2025.pdf\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: Jane\nHighlights:\nLeveraging the Augusta Rule for Business Owners \nIn reviewing this tax provision for shareholders and partners in entities (this would not be \navailable for sole proprietorships), we may consider the following opportunities for business \nowners: \n• Holiday Party or Retirement Party \n• Pool party or event for employees and their families \n• Special events such as product demonstrations or team building exercises \n• Planning meetings, “off-site” events, staff education events \n• Hosting customer events for sales promotions or goodwill \n• Providing temporary lodging for visiting remote employees or contractors Keep in mind the amount of rent paid by the company to the homeowner should be reasonable based on the cost to rent the same space from a local hotel, restaurant, or conference center. If the taxpayer is also deducting expenses for the business use of the home for a home office, applicable “rental events” should not be held in the same space in the home which is also used for the home office, this would cause the 14 or fewer days provision to fail. TAX NOTE: Business Owners who Rent Their Homes Not Eligible A business owner who rents their home and is not a homeowner, may not use the Augusta Rule, it applies exclusively to homeowners who own the property they are renting out. However, those business owners are generally still able for the deduction for home offices and may have bona fide tax deductions for some of the occasions listed above as an allocation of their personal rent. \nGig Economy: Short Term Rentals \nAn individual’s real estate rental activity from renting buildings, rooms or apartments, is \ngenerally reported on Form 1040, Schedule E, Part I, completing lines 1 and 2 for each \nproperty, including the street address for each property. Business entities C corporations, S \ncorporations, Partnerships and LLCs filing as Partnerships report this activity on Form 8825. \nOther attributes of each rental activity such as average number of days rented per year or \nwhether or not substantial services are provided may affect how the rental income is \ntreated, and which form it will be reported on for individual and business taxpayers. \nAverage Days in a Rental Period \nTo determine the average rental period for the year divide the total number of days rented by \nthe number of rental customers for the year. Note: If the same customer rents the property two \nor more times during the year, perhaps a stay during the summer and another stay during the \nholidays that counts as two rentals even though it’s the same customer. \nRounding of Average Days Rented Not Applicable \nDue to fact the regulations use “30 days or less” and “7 days or less’ to determine filing \nrequirements rounding the resulting average days calculation up won’t hurt or help. If the \ncalculated “average number of days” is 6.4 days that’s “7 days or less” but if the resulting \nnumber is 7.4 days, that is higher than “7 days or less” so the applicable filing \nrequirement will not apply.\n\n...\n\n• Provide losses for high income earners who do not qualify for real estate professional status. However, material participation required. Material Participation Regs To qualify for material participation one of the following must apply: \n• The taxpayer works 500 hours or more in the activity during the year \n• The taxpayer does all or nearly all of the work for the activity \n• The taxpayer works more than 100 hours in the activity for the year and no one else works more than you do \n• The activity is a significant participation activity (SPA), and the sum of the SPAs in which you work 100-500 hours exceeds 500 hours for the year \n• The taxpayer materially participated in the activity in any 5 of the previous 10 years \n• The activity is a personal service activity and the taxpayer materially participated in any of the previous 3 years \n• Based on the facts and circumstances, the taxpayer participated in the activity on a regular and continuous basis during the year. This rule applies if the taxpayer works at least 100 hours in the activity, no one else works more than you, and no one receives compensation for managing the property.\n\n...\n\nBusiness Entities Use Form 8825 as the Equivalent of Form 1040, Schedule E The “active participation” rules and application of “subject to Self-Employment Tax” rules are generally moot for real estate rental activity in an S corporation, but the activity would be reported on Form 8825.\n\n...\n\n• Lifetime Limit: There is no lifetime limit; the limit for the credit is determined on a yearly basis. For example, beginning in 2023, a taxpayer can claim the maximum Energy Efficient Home Improvement Credit allowed every year that eligible improvements are made. \n• Carryover of Unused Credit: No carryover to future years is permitted. Non-refundable Home Improvement Tax Credit, No Carry-Over, & AMT The Energy Efficient Home Improvement Credit is a nonrefundable personal tax credit. The credit can only be used to decrease or eliminate a tax liability. A taxpayer will not receive a tax refund for any amount that exceeds the taxpayer’s tax liability for the year. \nA taxpayer may not carry the credit forward. Thus, if a taxpayer does not have sufficient tax \nliability to claim all or a portion of the credit in the year in which the related property for which \nthe qualifying expenditure is placed in service, the unused amount of the credit may never be \nclaimed. \nA taxpayer who is subject to the AMT is eligible to claim the Residential Clean Energy Property \nCredit and may offset the AMT with those credits. \nQBI for Rental Real Estate \nQualified Business Income Deduction for Rental Real Estate \nDetermining Qualified Trades or Businesses for QBI \nThe content in italics in the paragraphs which follow is directly from the instructions for Form \n8995, Qualified Business Income Deduction Simplified Computation, and is included in the \nmaterial to support the position that QBI for the rental real estate activity engaged in by \nindividuals is eligible for the QBI deduction. Our commentary is indicated by the term NOTE: \nappended to the end of each paragraph. \nQuoting IRS Form 8995 Instructions: \nQualified trades and businesses include domestic trades or businesses for which the \ntaxpayer is allowed a deduction for ordinary and necessary business expenses under \n§162. However, trades or businesses conducted by corporations and the performance of \nservices as an employee aren’t qualified trades or businesses. NOTE: General expense \ndeductions for rental property such as insurance, advertising, repairs, and supplies are \nall business expenses under §162. Also, QBI will only apply to rental property located in \nthe U.S. or U.S. possessions. 13 \n\nGenerally, specified service trades or businesses (SSTBs) aren’t qualified trades or businesses. However, all or a part of the SSTB may be a qualified trade or business if taxable income is at or below the threshold or within the phase-in range. NOTE:\n\n13 Form 8995 Instructions\n\nCongress certainly has had the opportunity to specifically exclude or limit rental real estate activity from QBI, just as they’ve done with SSTBs, and have not excluded rental real estate activity from QBI in any legislation since TCJA.\n\nAs provided in §162, an activity qualifies as a trade or business if the primary purpose for engaging in the activity is for income or profit and the taxpayer is involved in the activity with continuity and regularity.\n\nMaterial participation under §469 isn’t required to qualify for the QBI deduction. Eligible taxpayers with income from a trade or business may be entitled to the QBI deduction if they otherwise satisfy the requirements of §199A. “The ownership and rental of real property may constitute a trade or business if it meets the standard described above.” NOTE: The preceding sentence in bold, italic quotes is directly from the IRS instructions.\n\n...\n\n3. Passive Activity Loss Limitations In most cases, all rental real estate activities (except those of certain real estate professionals, vacant land, and a self-rental with net income) are passive activities. For this purpose, a rental activity is an activity from which you receive income mainly for the use of tangible property, rather than for services. Deductions or losses from passive activities are limited. You generally cannot offset income, other than passive income, with losses from passive activities. Nor can you offset taxes on income, other than passive income, with credits resulting from passive activities. Any excess loss or credit is carried forward to the next tax year. Exception for Rental Real Estate With Active Participation If you or your spouse actively participated in a passive rental real estate activity, you may be able to deduct up to $25,000 of loss from the activity from your non-passive income. This special allowance is an exception to the general rule disallowing losses in excess of income from passive activities. Similarly, you may be able to offset credits from the activity against the tax on up to $25,000 of non-passive income after taking into account any losses allowed under this exception.\n\nActive or Passive Activity \nTaxpayers will indicate on their individual tax returns their status as an active or passive \nowner for each rental activity. \nActive participation. You actively participated in a rental real estate activity if you (or \nyour spouse) owned at least 10% of the rental property and you made management \ndecisions or arranged for others to provide services (such as repairs) in a significant \nand bona fide sense. Management decisions that may count as active participation \ninclude approving new tenants, deciding on rental terms, approving expenditures, and \nother similar decisions.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Passive Activities and Rental Real Estate Income Tax Issues WEBCAST - Washington Society of CPAs\nURL: https://www.wscpa.org/cpe/245911imp:passive-activities-and-rental-real-estate-income-tax-issues-webcast\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nrentals and passive activities, and what is a trade or business rental is under Section ... . How the PAL rules apply to rental real estate activities and investments in S corporations and partnerships. Definition of an activity and the activity\n\n---\n\nTitle: Understanding the Qualified Business Income Deduction in Income Lab - Income Lab\nURL: https://help.incomelaboratory.com/en_US/methodology/understanding-the-qualified-business-income-deduction-in-income-lab\nPublished: 2026-06-23T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n1. Eligible for QBI Deduction: If this is set to No, Income Lab excludes the item from the QBI deduction calculation. The income item will still be taxed according to its selected Tax Treatment, but it will not generate a QBI deduction. If this is set to Yes, Income Lab treats the item as potentially eligible QBI and shows the additional QBI settings. This is set to Yes by default for self-employment and pass-through business income, and to No for rental income. Rental income defaults to No because rental real estate does not automatically qualify for QBI. Some rentals qualify because they rise to the level of a trade or business or meet the IRS rental real estate safe harbor, but that determination is facts-and-circumstances based. Advisors should turn this setting on when they believe the rental activity qualifies. \n2. Client materially participates in this business: This setting tells Income Lab whether the client is actively involved in the business. Income Lab uses this primarily for the post-2025 active-business minimum QBI deduction rules. Beginning in 2026, certain taxpayers with at least a minimum amount of QBI from an active qualified trade or business may be eligible for a minimum QBI deduction. Material participation is relevant to determining whether the business is active for that purpose. The default is Yes for self-employment and pass-through business income. For self-employment income, the client often materially participates, so the default is Yes. This setting does not, by itself, determine whether the income is QBI-eligible. It affects specific QBI rules that depend on active participation. \n3. QBI amount is different from income amount: This setting controls whether Income Lab should use the income item’s amount as the QBI amount or ask for a separate QBI amount. If set to No, Income Lab uses the income amount as the QBI amount. If set to Yes, the modal displays a separate QBI Amount field. This matters because the amount of cash flow entered in a planning projection is not always the same as the tax QBI amount. This is Yes by default for pass-through business income and no otherwise. For pass-through business income, Income Lab defaults this to Yes because K-1 income, cash distributions, and QBI are often different. When available, advisors should use the QBI amount reported with the K-1’s Section 199A information. See below for examples.\n\n---\n\nTitle: 2019 Fed/State Tax Institute December 10‐11 & 13, 2019\nURL: https://revenuefiles.delaware.gov/2019/fedstate/4%20-%20IRS%20Tax%20Presentation%20-%20BLS.pdf\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: Christine.hambleton\nHighlights:\nTax Brackets – marginal & effective tax rates \n• Capital Gains Tax Rate \n• Medical Deduction \n• QBI Deduction \n• Special Allowance for Rental Real Estate for Active Participation \n• Medicare Surtax / Net Investment Income Tax \n• Medicare Part B and D\n\n---\n\nTitle: Rental Real Estate Webinar | TaxAct Professional\nURL: https://www.taxact.com/professional/rental-real-estate-webinar\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nrules, mixed- use properties, vacation rentals, commercial rentals, interest deduction criteria including interest tracing rules, ... , and the Business Interest Limitation. We'll also discuss material participation, grouping elections, depreciation and\n\n---\n\nTitle: Can a Rental Property Enterprise which qualifies for QBI treatment, also be used to offset Passive losses on another investment? | Community\nURL: https://ttlc.intuit.com/community/investors-landlords-12/can-a-rental-property-enterprise-which-qualifies-for-qbi-treatment-also-be-used-to-offset-passive-losses-on-another-investment-888428\nPublished: 2025-09-15T17:37:53.000Z\nAuthor: P PTR1 Level 2\nHighlights:\nthe special QBI rule for rental real estate does not transform it into a nonpassive activity.\n...\nmust demonstrate material participation in your real estate activities. This can be established by meeting one of the IRS's"
