        "output": "Title: How AI assistants file taxes with Terminal Tax | Terminal Tax\nURL: https://terminal.tax/integrations\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe service refuses taxpayer identification numbers and authentication values from an agent (every SSN or ITIN, the IP PIN, the Self-Select PIN, the prior-year PIN, the filing page's one-time code, driver's license or state ID values); the taxpayer types these protected values on the filing page. The taxpayer's own identity details are accepted from an agent in the packet's agentIdentity section, sealed beside the return and kept apart from it: legal name, date of birth, mailing address, occupation, dependents' names, email and mobile number, the refund or payment bank account, whether they filed a federal return last year and ever, and prior-year adjusted gross income. The section is required: a packet that lacks any required item is refused with one blocker per missing item, naming its path and the question for the taxpayer, and no filing link is issued until none is left. The filing page shows each value for the taxpayer to confirm or correct; no tool returns any of them. A packet that contains these protected values is refused. Source-form identifiers are separate and are sent in the packet: employer EINs, payer and lender EINs, K-1 partnership or corporation EINs, household-employer EINs and 5498-SA trustee EINs are fields of the forms they belong to, and the packet schema marks the ones each form requires, such as a W-2's employer EIN from box b. They appear in return review and the filing page does not ask the taxpayer to enter them, so a packet that omits a required one is returned with a blocker naming the field. SSN-shaped payer or lender numbers and an SSN-typed W-2G payer number remain refused. The taxpayer's signing PIN is the taxpayer's own signature, so the service does not receive it from an agent.\n\nThe return in the agent's packet carries only tax facts and filing choices. The agent can show the person a plain summary of the figures it prepared, such as wages and withholding from the person's own forms, so the person can check them before opening the filing page; that summary contains no identity values. Terminal Tax returns no computed figure (deduction, taxable income, tax, refund or amount owed) to the agent. The official return is rendered on the filing page for review before signing, so the agent does not build or present a Form 1040 or a substitute PDF.\n\n...\n\nWhere each packet fact comes from. A packet fact is one the taxpayer stated in the conversation or one a source document the taxpayer gave shows clearly. A question the taxpayer has not answered leaves its fact unknown, and an unknown fact is a question for the taxpayer, not a no, none, false or 0: an original rather than an amended return, no extension payment, no overpayment applied from last year, and no educator expenses or foreign accounts are each the taxpayer's own answer, not a default. A value printed clearly on the taxpayer's own form stands as printed without a second confirmation. A missing or ambiguous fact, two sources that disagree, and a reading of a document that changes the return (which form or box an amount belongs to, whether a form is final or corrected) are settled by the taxpayer's own short reply in the conversation before the packet is sent; a general acknowledgement such as \"ok\" or \"looks right\" settles no particular fact. Credit amounts (the earned income credit, child tax credit, additional child tax credit, credit for other dependents and American opportunity credit) are figured from the confirmed facts with the tax year's official IRS worksheet and table, not asked of the taxpayer; Terminal Tax files them as given and checks only mechanical limits and the schedules' own arithmetic. Terminal Tax reads only the packet: it cannot see the conversation or tell a stated fact from a filled-in guess, so an absent required fact comes back as a blocker naming the question for the taxpayer, while a plausible value nobody stated reaches the filing page as though the taxpayer had given it.\n\n---\n\nTitle: E-file your tax return with the IRS from your AI assistant: hosted MCP server, no tax website | Terminal Tax\nURL: https://terminal.tax/e-file-from-your-ai-assistant\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n: your assistant passes the facts from your forms and your choices, Terminal Tax computes the return, ... sign it and authorize payment on one private filing page before Terminal Tax e-files it with the IRS. Ask your assistant to e-file your tax return\n\n---\n\nTitle: N/A\nURL: https://terminal.tax/llms.txt\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nTerminal Tax is tax software for e-filing an individual income tax return. The person gives the facts from their tax forms and makes every choice through their own AI agent; Terminal Tax computes the return deterministically and validates it mechanically. The person reviews the complete return, enters their identity details and signs on one hosted filing page; Terminal Tax then e-files it. It does not choose tax treatments or give tax advice.\n\n...\n\nThe service refuses taxpayer identification numbers and authentication values from an agent (every SSN or ITIN, the IP PIN, the Self-Select PIN, the prior-year PIN, the filing page's one-time code, driver's license or state ID values); the taxpayer types these protected values on the filing page. The taxpayer's own identity details are accepted from an agent in the packet's agentIdentity section, sealed beside the return and kept apart from it: legal name, date of birth, mailing address, occupation, dependents' names, email and mobile number, the refund or payment bank account, whether they filed a federal return last year and ever, and prior-year adjusted gross income. The section is required: a packet that lacks any required item is refused with one blocker per missing item, naming its path and the question for 